  "Source": "M. A. Kats and F. Capasso, \"Optical absorbers based on strong interference in ultra-thin films,\" Laser Photonics Rev., 1-15 (2016) (Early View; volume and final pages not printed in this copy). doi:10.1002/lpor.201600098 - Sec. 3, p. 3, first paragraph (example for Fig. 1(c)): n1 = n3 = 1, lambda0 = 900 nm, h = 10 nm, Re[n2] = Im[n2] = 3.8",
  "Notes": "Ultra-thin lossy film between identical lossless media (n1 = n3 = 1) tuned to the A = 0.5 absorption limit at normal incidence\nSpecification: Maximum absorptance A = 0.5 of a film with h << lambda between identical transparent media (condition Re[n2] ~ Im[n2] ~ sqrt((n1+n3) lambda0 / (4 pi d))).\nReported: A = 0.5 at lambda0 = 900 nm, normal incidence (Sec. 3, p. 3; Fig. 1(c) shows the absorption map vs n and kappa for h = 10 nm with maximum near n = kappa = 3.8). A_limit = 0.5 for any n1 = n3 and any angle for both polarisations in the thin-film limit.\nNotes: Hypothetical material: n2 is a single complex number at 900 nm (paper's convention n + i kappa, so k = 3.8 positive). Free-standing film in air on both sides. The closed form Re = Im = sqrt(2*900/(4 pi 10)) = 3.785, printed as 3.8, so A computed with 3.8 + 3.8i should be very close to (just under) 0.5.\nLayers from Sec. 3, p. 3, first paragraph (example for Fig. 1(c)): n1 = n3 = 1, lambda0 = 900 nm, h = 10 nm, Re[n2] = Im[n2] = 3.8; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours A 0.501 at 900 nm; paper A 0.5.",
